<p>Philomena was born to French immigrants John and Magdalena (Criqui) Knaus on August 12,1854 in Buffalo, NY. By 1860, the Knauses settled in Stearns County, MN by way of Iowa. Henry was born to Joseph and Elizabeth (Bruining) Yunck in 1855 in Taos, Missouri. By 1865, their family also moved to Stearns County. Eventually, Philomena and Henry met and married on August 9, 1873. Their first child Marie was born in 1874. The young couple moved to Bismarck, Dakota Territory in 1878 and operated a boarding house at the Bismarck steamboat landing. Lodging was in short supply with the start of construction of the Missouri River bridge for the Northern Pacific Railroad. Philomena cooked meals for the bridge building crew and Henry worked as a laborer on the landing. And their second child Joseph was born.</p>

<p>They crossed the Missouri River in 1879 and started a new business in the new city of Mandan. By January of 1880, the Yuncks celebrated the grand opening of the Merchants Hotel situated across the street and just down the block from the first Northern Pacific Railroad depot (located at the T-intersection of today's Main Street and Collins Avenue.)</p>

